is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: RIP (RIPv2) routing advertisements for Location C’s LANs. Mechanism: Location C’s subnets (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24) are not being injected into the RIP domain, so some routers never learn them and others lose them when stale entries age out. From Location A this looks like “routes to C” appearing and disappearing as devices time out or use inconsistent paths.

Localization: Router6 (Location C edge). Evidence shows router6 runs RIP only on 10.50.1.0/24: its FRR config has “router rip … network 10.0.0.0/8” only, while its LANs are 172.18.50.254/24 (eth0) and 172.18.51.254/24 (eth1). Router4 (Location A edge) has no route to 172.18.51.0/24 in its routing table, and pc11 (192.168.200.11, behind router4) cannot ping pc15 (172.18.51.15). Router3 reaches C via static routes (“172.18.50.0/24 via 10.50.1.2”, “172.18.51.0/24 via 10.50.1.2”), but these are not redistributed into RIP, so downstream routers like router4 never learn them.

Root cause: Router6’s RIP is misconfigured to exclude Location C LANs, so 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 are not advertised into the RIP domain, leading to route loss/aging and intermittent reachability from Location A.

Corrective fix: On router6, enable RIP on the LANs (e.g., under “router rip” add “network 172.18.0.0/16” or “redistribute connected” with version 2). Alternatively, on router3, “redistribute static” into RIP to propagate its static routes. After enabling proper advertisement, verify router4 learns 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 via RIP and that pc11->pc15 pings are stable. Remove any ad-hoc static routes to avoid inconsistencies.
=== END ===